# NSG Phase 2c (antenna) — per-cell panel orientation, and the element model
# that makes it mean anything.
#
# ── CORRECTION to the "ANTENNA: WON'T DO" verdict (2026-07-14) ──────────────
# That verdict read: "mmWave gNBs use UniformPlanarArray 8x8 + beamforming, i.e.
# the beam is STEERED at the UE; the config's azimuth/tilt are a different
# antenna paradigm and forcing them in would REDUCE fidelity.
# SetEnbPhasedArrayModelAttribute is helper-level = global anyway (no per-cell
# antenna)." Audited at the source; it is wrong in one half and right for the
# wrong reason in the other.
#
# 1. PER-CELL ANTENNAS EXIST. MmWaveHelper::InstallEnbDevice is nothing but a
#    loop over InstallSingleEnbDevice, and each of those calls
#    m_enbPhasedArrayModelFactory.Create<PhasedArrayModel>()
#    (mmwave-helper.cc). The FACTORY is global; the INSTANCES are per device.
#    Installing one node at a time, re-setting the factory in between, gives
#    every cell its own panel. That is what this patch does.
#
# 2. ORIENTATION IS INERT — BECAUSE OF THE ELEMENT, NOT THE BEAMFORMING.
#    UniformPlanarArray::GetElementFieldPattern DOES apply the 3GPP TR 38.901
#    GCS->LCS rotation by m_alpha (bearing) / m_beta (downtilt). But
#    PhasedArrayModel's AntennaElement attribute defaults to
#    IsotropicAntennaModel and the mmWave helper never overrides it, and
#    rotating a lattice of ISOTROPIC elements cancels out exactly. So the
#    conclusion "azimuth/tilt do nothing" was correct; the reason given was not,
#    and the reason is what made it look unfixable.
#
# 3. THE FIX WAS ALREADY IN THE TREE. src/antenna/model/three-gpp-antenna-model
#    (65 deg vertical/horizontal HPBW, SLA_V, 30 dB max attenuation) ships with
#    ns-3. Point AntennaElement at it and the panel really gates gain.
#
# ── What is wired ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# * radio.antenna.azimuth_deg / tilt_deg (+ massive_mimo.rows/cols) ->
#   gnb_antenna -> NSG_GNB_ANTENNA -> --gnbAntennaFile, ';'-sep per gNB
#   'azimuth_deg,tilt_deg,rows,cols' aligned with gnbPositionsFile. Azimuth
#   travels in the CONFIG's compass convention (0 = North, clockwise) and the
#   scenario converts it to ns-3's phi (0 = +x/East, counter-clockwise),
#   wrapped into (-pi, pi] because BearingAngle's checker rejects anything else.
# * settings.antenna_element -> --antennaElement=isotropic|3gpp. Default
#   isotropic = the inherited engine, so existing runs are unchanged. The two
#   settings are NOT comparable: the 3GPP element adds ~8 dBi of boresight gain.
# * A cell whose panel is all-default (azimuth 0, tilt 0, 8x8) emits no spec at
#   all, so an untouched config still takes the original bulk install path.
#
# ── Measured (2026-07-26, engines/nsoran/test_antenna_2c.py) ────────────────
# 3 cells in a line, 6 UEs ~350 m SOUTH of them, 50 Mbps/UE offered:
#
#   A  isotropic, boresight AT the UEs (S)          9.77 -> 6.31 Mbps/UE
#   B  isotropic, boresight AWAY (N, 180 off)               6.95   (+10.1%)
#   C  3GPP,      boresight AT the UEs (S)                  9.77
#   D  3GPP,      boresight AWAY (N, 180 off)               3.96   (-59.5%)
#   E  3GPP,      AT the UEs but tilted 80 deg UP           2.85   (-70.9%)
#
#   * Element model alone: 6.31 -> 9.77 Mbps/UE (+54.8%) — the TR 38.901
#     element's boresight gain. This is why the two settings can't be compared.
#   * Isotropic ignores orientation: +10.1% IS the noise floor (see below), not
#     an effect. The old verdict's observation was right.
#   * 3GPP makes azimuth real: -59.5% straight behind the panel.
#   * Downtilt is real too, and is the LAYOUT-INDEPENDENT half — whichever
#     direction a UE lies in, a panel aimed at the sky puts it in the vertical
#     pattern's attenuation. That is the form the fidelity harness uses.
#
#   FIDELITY HARNESS lever ("2c antenna aimed at sky"), on its own 5-cell /
#   10-UE baseline with the demand raised to 50 Mbps/UE for both of its runs:
#   12.56 -> 10.36 Mbps/UE (-17.5%). Same direction, much smaller than the
#   dedicated test's -70.9%, because that baseline sits at +8.75 dB SINR (the
#   flat part of the rate curve) with UEs spread all round, while the dedicated
#   geometry sits at -2.17 dB with every UE on one side. -17.5% is only ~2x the
#   noise floor, so treat the harness entry as a WIRING check and
#   test_antenna_2c.py as the magnitude proof. Left at 10 Mbps/UE the same
#   lever read -6%, i.e. inside the floor — a green light for the wrong reason.
#
# ── Traps this cost time on ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# * MEASURE ON A LINK-LIMITED RUN. The first attempt offered 10 Mbps/UE and
#   measured 10.69 vs 10.41 Mbps/UE — the UEs were DEMAND-limited, so a link
#   that got much worse showed a 2.6% change. Raise the offered load until
#   throughput is the link's answer, not the traffic generator's.
# * THE NOISE FLOOR IS ~8-10%. Two runs that should be IDENTICAL (isotropic,
#   two orientations) differ that much, because each run reaches a slightly
#   different sim span in its wall-clock window and averages a different number
#   of samples. A 90 deg rotation (~23 dB of element attenuation, partly
#   recovered by the beamformer) sat INSIDE that floor and read as "no effect".
#   Design the check to land far outside it — straight behind the panel, where
#   the element hits its 30 dB front-to-back cap.
# * L3servingSINR DOES NOT MOVE. It read -2.17 dB to two decimals in all five
#   runs above, including the pair whose throughput differed by 55%. Whatever it
#   reports, it is not tracking the antenna; use throughput (and PRB) instead.
